  "prediction": "**Multiple Arrests Made in Robeson County Arson Investigation**\n\nIn a shocking turn of events, 10 volunteer firefighters from the Fairmont and Orrum Fire Departments in Robeson County, North Carolina, have been arrested and charged with intentionally setting fires to abandoned homes and woods over a two-year period. The investigation, led by the Robeson County Sheriff's Office, State Bureau of Investigation, Alcohol, Tobacco and Firearms, and the NC Forestry Service, has resulted in approximately 90 arson-related charges, with more expected.\n\nThe arrested individuals include:\n\n1. **Melquan Williams**, 21, of Fairmont, charged with one count of felony arson and one count of felony conspiracy, with a bond set at $200,000.\n2. **Austin Seth Hunt**, 23, of Rowland, charged with six counts of felony setting fire to grass, brush or woods, two counts of felony arson burning a church, uninhabited house or store, and five counts of felony conspiracy, with a bond set at $200,000.\n3. **Ashley Marie Oxendine**, 18, of Orrum, charged with two counts of felony setting fire to grass, brush or woods and two counts of felony conspiracy, with a bond set at $100,000.\n4. **John Austin Huggins**, 17, of Fairmont, charged with two counts of felony setting fire to grass, brush or woods, two counts of felony arson burning a church, uninhabitable house or store and four counts of felony conspiracy, with a bond set at $100,000.\n5. **Jordan Scott**, 19, of Fairmont, charged with four counts of felony setting fire to grass, brush or woods, five counts of arson burning a church, uninhabited house or store and nine counts of felony conspiracy, with a bond set at $100,000.\n6. **Kenny Caulder Jr.**, 19, of Fairmont, charged with six counts of felony arson burning a church, uninhabited house or store, two counts of felony setting fire to grass, brush or woods and seven counts of felony conspiracy, with a bond set at $100,000.\n7. **Willie Fuentes Strickland**, 42, of Fairmont, charged with two counts of felony setting fire to grass, brush or woods, one count of felony arson burning a church, uninhabited house or store and three counts of felony conspiracy, with a bond set at $100,000.\n8.",
